# Strict-Transport-Security tells browsers to refuse plain HTTP for this host.
# Six months, and deliberately WITHOUT includeSubDomains or preload: both are
# hard to walk back, includeSubDomains would commit every current and future
# subdomain of nextacts.xyz sight unseen, and preload is effectively permanent.
# Raise to a year and add the others once this has run without incident.
#
# Content-Security-Policy comes in two, which is intentional.
#
# The ENFORCED one is what the site can honour today. It stops an injected
# <script src="evil"> from loading, an injected <base> from rewriting every
# relative URL, an injected form from posting off-site, plugin/object
# embedding, and framing by another origin. It does NOT stop an injected
# INLINE script, because five pages -- /blog, /coverage, /offer, /reporting and
# /severance -- carry hand-written inline scripts and 'unsafe-inline' is what
# keeps them running. That is a real limit, not a formality: /coverage and
# /severance are the two pages that read documents a reader pastes in.
#
# The REPORT-ONLY one drops 'unsafe-inline' from script-src and nothing else,
# so the console reports exactly which inline scripts would have to move into
# files before the strict policy can be enforced. It cannot break a page.
#
# style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' in BOTH: /severance alone has 62 inline style
# attributes, and reporting them would bury the script violations that matter.
#
# frame-src names Substack because /blog embeds their subscribe form. It is the
# only external origin the site loads anything from; every other external URL
# in the markup is a link, which CSP does not govern.
#
# ⚠️ MEASURED 2026-08-20, and it decides how far this can ever go: CLOUDFLARE
# ITSELF INJECTS AN INLINE SCRIPT into every response -- the bot-detection
# bootstrap that sets window.__CF$cv$params and loads
# /cdn-cgi/challenge-platform/scripts/jsd/main.js. It is not in our build, which
# is why reading the repository does not reveal it, and it carries a token that
# CHANGES ON EVERY REQUEST (r:'a2e4c97abc2b67a6', then '...c09ca0653', then
# '...d1bd6238f' on three consecutive fetches). Its hash therefore differs every
# time, so no hash-based policy can ever cover it.
#
# So moving our own inline scripts into files would NOT let script-src go
# strict. While Cloudflare injects that script the only options are
# 'unsafe-inline' or turning the Cloudflare feature off, and that is a trade
# against bot protection rather than a code change. The report-only policy is
# still worth keeping: it is what surfaced this, and it will show any NEW inline
# script we add ourselves.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15768000
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://nextacts.substack.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://nextacts.substack.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The employer files change four times a year, when DOL publishes a new
# quarterly release -- and a new release is written to a new path, because the
# release id is part of the URL (see stateDataPath in src/lib/sponsor-search.mjs).
# A given path therefore never changes content, which is what makes `immutable`
# safe here. Previously these were served must-revalidate, so every reader paid
# a round trip to the origin for a file that had not moved in months.
#
# The `:release` segment is load-bearing, NOT decoration. A bare
# /data/sponsors/* also matches the unversioned paths this layout replaced --
# and Cloudflare Pages keeps serving deleted assets from its own cache, so
# /data/sponsors/CA.json still answers 200 with the old file. Marking THAT
# immutable would freeze a superseded quarter in readers' browsers for a year.
# Anything without a release segment must fall through to the default.
/data/sponsors/:release/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
